# What must never be uploaded to Vercel by `vercel deploy`.
#
# ## Read this before editing
#
# `.vercelignore` applies to **CLI uploads only**. A Git-integration deploy
# clones the repository, so what it contains is decided by `.gitignore` and by
# nothing here — Vercel's own docs are explicit that the default exclusions
# "are only relevant when using Vercel CLI".
#
# That split is why this file matters more than it looks. Everything large or
# copyrighted in this project is already gitignored, so a Git deploy is safe by
# construction. A CLI deploy is not: `vercel deploy` uploads the working
# directory, gitignored files included. Without the entries below, one
# `npx vercel --prod` publishes 300 MB of NCERT textbooks and CBSE papers to a
# public URL — the exact thing PERMISSIONS.md says must not happen until a
# permission request that has still not been sent.
#
# The prevailing rule: if a path is in `.gitignore` because of copyright or
# size, it belongs here too.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copyright. Not ours to publish. See PERMISSIONS.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NCERT chapter PDFs, ~264 MB compressed. Republishing without permission is
# what NCERT's own press release calls infringement.
public/ncert/
data/ncert-original/

# CBSE sample papers and marking schemes, 78 MB. A teacher reaches these
# through object storage instead — `npx tsx scripts/publish-schemes.ts` — never
# from a public path. See DEPLOY.md.
public/papers/
data/cbse/

# Past-paper page scans: ~1.3 GB and ~20,000 files. The archive they come from
# permits personal download and forbids redistribution. Build with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PYQ_ARCHIVE=off so the app does not generate routes into them.
public/pyqs/

# NCERT Exemplar, same position as the textbooks.
public/exemplar/

# Pipeline inputs. Nothing serves these; they exist so the scripts can re-run.
data/curriculum/
data/prelims/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and local state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vercel's default exclusions cover `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` but NOT a
# plain `.env`, which is where this project's DATABASE_URL and SESSION_SECRET
# live. Environment variables belong in the Vercel dashboard.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Students' photographed answer sheets and voice notes, written by the local
# storage driver. Uploading them would be the single worst thing in this file.
.storage/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output — Vercel builds from source
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.next` and `node_modules` are excluded by Vercel automatically; `out/` is a
# leftover from when this was a static export and no longer produced.
out/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `scripts/` is deliberately NOT excluded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It used to be, and that was a real bug: `npm run build` begins with
# `node scripts/copy-pdf-worker.mjs`, which copies pdf.js's worker into
# `public/`. Vercel runs that build command on its own builders from whatever
# was uploaded, so on a CLI deploy the directory was absent and the build failed
# at its first command. A Git deploy was unaffected — `.vercelignore` does not
# apply there — which is exactly the kind of asymmetry that gets diagnosed as
# "works from GitHub, broken from my laptop".
#
# The content pipeline in `scripts/` still never runs on Vercel. It is a few
# hundred kilobytes of source; uploading it costs nothing and not uploading it
# costs the build.
